\DOC LOCAL_ADD_4_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_ADD_4_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances a symbolic {eventually} execution goal across one inlined
modular-addition subroutine, replacing the current state variable with a
successor and adding a characterisation of the result written to memory.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} offset/index selecting which call site or
variant of the addition block to step over; the second is a {string list}
naming the result and the two operands, used both to name the new result
variable and to phrase the arithmetic consequence derived about it. The
conclusion is rewritten to the same {eventually} statement applied to a fresh
successor state variable. In the assumptions, every fact about the old state —
program counter, register and memory reads, {bytes_loaded}, and the accumulated
{MAYCHANGE ... s0 s} — is removed and replaced by the corresponding fact about
the new state, with the program counter advanced and the {MAYCHANGE} relation
extended by one further component for the memory block written. Two further
assumptions are added: a read assumption giving the contents of that block as a
fresh variable {t}, and a conditional arithmetic assumption of the form {u1 <
... /\ u2 < ... ==> t = u1 + u2}.
{
   A ?- eventually x86 (\s'. P s') s
   =================================================  LOCAL_ADD_4_TAC n ["res"; "op1"; "op2"]
   A' ?- eventually x86 (\s'. P s') s_new
   ```
   where `A'` is `A` with the old-state facts replaced by their new-state counterparts (program counter advanced, `MAYCHANGE` extended) together with the new read and conditional arithmetic assumptions.
}

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of step, always on goals that
are symbolic-execution states of this form with assumptions already present.

\ENDDOC
